course: biology
lesson: 132
title: Finding Nemo: real clownfish and sea anemones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Clownfish really do live inside sea anemones, whose waving tentacles are full of stings that kill other small fish.
explain: A clownfish is safe because a layer of slimy mucus covers its skin, and it touches the anemone carefully at first while its mucus slowly changes, until the stings stop firing at it.
explain: It is a good deal for both: the anemone keeps the clownfish safe from bigger fish, and the clownfish cleans the anemone and chases off fish that would nibble it.
explain: Real clownfish families are stranger than the film: the biggest fish is the mother, and if she is lost, the father turns into a female, so a real Marlin would have become Nemo's new mum.
fact: A sea anemone looks like a flower but is an animal, a relative of jellyfish and corals, that catches small animals with stinging tentacles and pushes them into a mouth in its middle.
fact: Some anemones can slowly glide along a rock on their base.
fact: Real clownfish spend nearly all their lives within a few metres of their anemone, because leaving its tentacles is dangerous for a small fish.
fact: Clownfish lay eggs on a rock beside their anemone, and the father fans and guards them, which keeps fresh water with oxygen flowing over them; they hatch in about a week, usually at night.
fact: Newly hatched clownfish drift in the open sea as tiny larvae for about one or two weeks, then find a reef by its smell and crackling sound and swim to it.
fact: Fish can live with an uneven fin from birth or injury and swim with their other fins and tail, and many fish can regrow damaged fins.
fact: There are about 30 kinds of clownfish: some orange with white stripes, others dark, pink or striped differently, and each lives with particular kinds of anemones.
fact: Clownfish make popping and clicking sounds to talk to each other.
fact: In a clownfish group the biggest fish is the female and the next biggest is the male; if the female is lost, the male becomes a female and the next fish becomes the male.
fact: After the film many clownfish were taken from reefs for pet tanks, which left too few to lay eggs in some wild groups; most clownfish sold in shops today are bred in tanks.
fact: Coral reefs are built by tiny animals called coral polyps that make hard homes of limestone, and tiny algae living inside the coral give it food and colour.
fact: When the sea gets too warm, coral throws out its algae and turns white, which is called bleaching.
fact: The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is the biggest coral reef system in the world, about 2,300 kilometres long, made of thousands of reefs and islands.
exercise: Why is a clownfish not stung by its anemone? | A layer of slimy mucus protects its skin | It has a hard shell like a crab under its bright scales | It is too fast for the stings | The anemone is asleep all day
exercise: What does the clownfish give the anemone in return for shelter? | It cleans it and chases away fish that eat it | It feeds it fresh seaweed every morning and every evening | It carries it to new rocks | It sings to it at night
exercise: What is a sea anemone? | An animal related to jellyfish and corals | A flower that grows on the rocks under the sea | A kind of seaweed | A kind of sponge-like plant
exercise: How does an anemone catch its food? | With stinging tentacles that push food into its mouth | With roots that suck food from the rock | By making its own food from sunlight, just like a garden plant | By chasing fish across the reef
exercise: How far do real clownfish usually go from their anemone? | Only a few metres | Across the whole ocean every year | About a hundred kilometres | To a new reef every week
exercise: Who guards and fans the clownfish eggs? | The father | The mother | The anemone | Nobody, the eggs are left alone
exercise: Why does the father fan the eggs with his fins? | To keep fresh water with oxygen flowing over them | To dry them out in the warm sun near the surface | To keep sharks away with wind | To make them hatch the same day
exercise: About when do clownfish eggs hatch? | After about a week, usually at night | After a whole year, in the spring | After an hour or so, at noon | After a month, in a storm
exercise: What do newly hatched baby clownfish do? | Drift in the open sea for one or two weeks, then find a reef | Stay inside the egg for months | Live inside the anemone with their father from the very first day | Ride on their father's back
exercise: How do baby clownfish find a reef? | By its smell and its crackling sound | By following the Moon | By reading the colours of the sky | By following their mother all the way home
exercise: Can a fish with one small fin survive? | Yes, it can swim with its other fins and tail | No, it can never swim at all and sinks to the bottom | No, it must stay in a tank | Yes, but only in fresh water
exercise: How many kinds of clownfish are there? | About 30 | Only one | About 3 | About 3,000
exercise: Which fish is the biggest in a clownfish group? | The female, the mother | The male, the father | The youngest baby | They are all exactly the same size
exercise: What happens if the female clownfish is lost? | The male turns into a female | The group leaves the anemone | The babies grow up alone with no parents | A female arrives from far away at once
exercise: Where do most clownfish sold in shops come from today? | They are bred in tanks | They are caught on the Great Barrier Reef | They are made in a laboratory from coral | They swim into shops from the sea
exercise: What gives coral its colour? | Tiny algae living inside it | Paint washed in by rivers | Sunlight bouncing off sand | The fish that eat it
exercise: What is coral bleaching? | Coral throws out its algae in warm water and turns white | Coral is washed by the waves | Coral grows lots of new white branches every spring | Coral changes colour every night
exercise: About how long is the Great Barrier Reef? | About 2,300 kilometres | About 23 kilometres | About 230 metres | About 23,000 kilometres
test: An anemone home has one female, one male and three small clownfish. A diver's net takes the female. How many females will the group soon have? | 1 | 0 | 2 | 4
test: «Marlin lost his wife so he's Nemo's dad forever, right» What would happen in real clownfish? | Marlin would turn into a female and become the mother | Marlin would stay a male and find a new wife far away | Marlin would leave the anemone for ever | Nemo would turn into the mother at once
test: «sea anemone's a flower innit, it's on the reef like a garden» What is right? | No, it is an animal that catches food with stinging tentacles | Yes, it is a pretty sea flower that needs bees to carry its pollen | Yes, it is a kind of seaweed | No, it is a kind of rock
test: In SpongeBob, fish live in houses and drive boats. What do real clownfish actually live in? | The stinging tentacles of a sea anemone | Hollow pineapples on the sandy sea floor | Holes dug in the sand | Sunken ships only
test: «my nan's clownfish is black not orange, it's sick then?» What is right? | Probably fine, some kinds of clownfish are dark | Yes, all clownfish must be orange, or they are ill | Yes, it has turned black from old age | It is not a clownfish at all
test: «Nemo swims halfway across the ocean, clownfish do that all the time» What is right? | Real clownfish stay within a few metres of their anemone | Yes, they swim thousands of kilometres across the ocean every year | Yes, they migrate with whales | No, clownfish cannot swim at all
test: What would happen if the sea around a reef stayed too warm for weeks? | The coral could throw out its algae and turn white | The coral would grow faster and brighter in the warm water | The clownfish would turn into females | Nothing at all would happen
test: «why'd anyone care if people take a few clownfish off a reef for tanks» What is right? | Taking many from one place leaves too few to lay eggs | Clownfish are happier in tanks, as there are no sharks there | The reef has endless clownfish | Clownfish grow back from coral
test: «shut up, fish don't talk» What is true about clownfish? | They make popping and clicking sounds to each other | They are completely silent, like all other fish | They talk only to the sharks, to warn them off | They whistle like birds
test: Why does a clownfish touch its anemone carefully at first? | Its mucus changes until the anemone no longer stings it | It is checking whether the anemone is ripe enough to eat | It is waking the anemone up | It is asking another fish's permission
test: «big reef's in Australia right, the Nemo one» What is right? | Yes, the Great Barrier Reef, the biggest coral reef system in the world | No, it is in the Arctic | Yes, but it is only a few metres long, so you can swim round it | No, it is made by people
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protection of the clownfish from the stinging tentacles of its host. | A layer of mucus upon the skin of the fish prevents the discharge of the stings | The fish is protected by an external shell of considerable hardness | The anemone voluntarily refrains from feeding during daylight | The fish evades each sting by superior speed of movement
exam: Identify the consequence which ensues within a clownfish group upon the loss of the female. | The largest male undergoes transformation into a female | The group abandons the anemone in its entirety | A female is recruited at once from a distant reef | No further eggs are produced thereafter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sea anemone? | It is an animal related to jellyfish and corals which captures prey by means of stinging tentacles | It is a flowering plant of the sea floor which requires pollination | It is a variety of seaweed which manufactures food from light | It is a mineral formation produced by coral polyps
exam: Ascertain the phenomenon designated as coral bleaching. | The expulsion by coral of its algae in excessively warm water, with consequent whitening | The washing of coral by waves of unusual force during violent storms at sea | The growth of new white branches in the spring season | The nightly alteration of coral colour by moonlight
